DTEND;TZID=Europe/London:20261124T190000
SUMMARY:Can Courts Save the Environment Without Weakening Democracy? Rethin
 king Environmental Judicial Activism in South Asia
LOCATION:The Umney Theatre\, Robinson College\, Grange Road\, CB3 9AN
URL:https://talks.cam.ac.uk/talk/index/274135/
CATEGORIES:Law,Politics
DESCRIPTION:What's On at Robinson College\nTopics: Law\, Politics\nDetails:
  https://talks.cam.ac.uk/talk/index/274135/\nIn his lecture\, Dr Jashim Ch
 owdhury looks into the matter of the e nvironmental constitutionalism whic
 h has empowered courts to respond where political institutions appear unwi
 lling or unable to act. In India\, Pakistan and Bangladesh\, however\, thi
 s expansion of judicial authority raises a neglected question: can environ
 mentally progressive judgments nevertheless be democratically deficient? \
 n\n Drawing on his forthcoming book\, Dr Chowdhury examines a pattern of e
 nvironmental adjudication that is normatively ambitious but frequently les
 s inclusive\, less democratic and more combative than its objectives requi
 re. In his lecture he argues that the central challenge is not choosing be
 tween judicial activism and restraint\, but reimagining the relationship b
 etween courts\, legislatures\, governments and affected communities. Bring
 ing South Asian jurisprudence into conversation with deliberative de
END:VEVENT
